{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdZjnrwWfUerPtvp2KAS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the repaired candidate, `personal_complement_symmetry_aversion`, and it passes the self-check (`OK`, smoke-fit ELPD-LOO −3459.7). Convergence, the thing that sank the last attempt, is still untested: the self-check runs one short chain and can't report R-hat. The first real test will be admission's full fit, and this slot gets no further attempt.\n\n**Why I changed the mechanism instead of patching it.** The cached fits from the last attempt explain the rejection. The model gave each person an overall \"decisiveness\" that scaled every cue at once. That trades off against the shared cue weights and the personal guessing rate, so `sigma_dec`, `sigma_lapse` and the shared weights mixed badly (bulk ESS roughly 80–170). It also scored ELPD-LOO −3494.5 against the incumbent's −3470.5, so a version that converged would still have lost.\n\n**The new change.** I kept the incumbent `complement_symmetry_aversion` exactly and changed one thing: the weight on complement symmetry (antisymmetry) is now each person's own, drawn from a population (non-centred: `mu_chi + sigma_chi * z_chi`, 400 slots), instead of shared. I picked this cue because, across the 380 stimulus pairs, the left-minus-right antisymmetry difference correlates only weakly with the cues that already have personal weights (−0.6 with imbalance, −0.33 with longest streak). That avoids the person-level trade-offs that broke the last fit. The incumbent's own full fit converged cleanly (every R-hat 1.00, ESS above 4,500). It also targets the critique that survived FDR: people differ in how often they agree with the majority more than the incumbent predicts.\n\n**Two risks:**\n- **Novelty gate:** averaged over participants, its predictions may sit close to the incumbent's, since only pairs with an antisymmetric sequence (37% of pairs) change. It needs an RMSE of at least 0.002 on the novelty pool; I didn't measure that.\n- **PSIS-LOO reliability:** the smoke fit flagged 8.9% of trials with Pareto k > 0.5. That's common with 100 draws, and the incumbent's estimate is reliable at full length, but it isn't confirmed here.\n\nFiles are in `experiment3/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_3_repair_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":221,"cache_read_input_tokens":104056,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":221,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":727,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8edc10bc-bdbf-404a-a106-498d19bdb5b7","uuid":"07ede3db-992a-4d78-95ab-7d57ad03910c","timestamp":"2026-10-02T14:26:18.304Z","request_id":"req_011CfdZjnSP2qKv21f7Zhs5A"}
